    "response": " To solve the problem, we need to determine the amount of salt in the tank after 20 minutes. Let's denote the amount of salt in the tank at time \\( t \\) by \\( S(t) \\) (in kg).\n\nFirst, we calculate the rate at which salt is entering the tank. The brine flow rate is 3 L/min and the concentration of salt in the brine is 0.5 kg/L. Therefore, the rate at which salt is entering the tank is:\n\\[\n\\text{Rate in} = 3 \\, \\text{L/min} \\times 0.5 \\, \\text{kg/L} = 1.5 \\, \\text{kg/min}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the rate at which salt is leaving the tank. The solution flow rate out of the tank is 2 L/min. The concentration of salt in the tank at any time \\( t \\) is \\( \\frac{S(t)}{V(t)} \\), where \\( V(t) \\) is the volume of the solution in the tank at time \\( t \\). Initially, the tank contains 100 L of pure water, and the volume changes at a rate of \\( 3 \\, \\text{L/min} - 2 \\, \\text{L/min} = 1 \\, \\text{L/min} \\). Therefore, the volume of the solution in the tank at time \\( t \\) is:\n\\[\nV(t) = 100 + t\n\\]\nSo, the rate at which salt is leaving the tank is:\n\\[\n\\text{Rate out} = 2 \\, \\text{L/min} \\times \\frac{S(t)}{100 + t} = \\frac{2S(t)}{100 + t} \\, \\text{kg/min}\n\\]\n\nThe rate of change of the amount of salt in the tank is the difference between the rate at which salt is entering and the rate at which salt is leaving:\n\\[\n\\frac{dS}{dt} = 1.5 - \\frac{2S(t)}{100 + t}\n\\]\nThis is a first-order linear differential equation. To solve it, we use an integrating factor. The integrating factor is given by:\n\\[\n\\mu(t) = e^{\\int -\\frac{2}{100 + t} \\, dt} = e^{-2 \\ln(100 + t)} = (100 + t)^{-2}\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by the integrating factor, we get:\n\\[\n(100 + t)^{-2} \\frac{dS}{dt} - 2(100 + t)^{-3} S = 1.5(100 + t)^{-2}\n\\]\nThe left-hand side is the derivative of \\( (100 + t)^{-2} S \\):\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt} \\left[ (100 + t)^{-2} S \\right] = 1.5(100 + t)^{-2}\n\\]\nIntegrating both sides with respect to \\( t \\), we get:\n\\[\n(100 + t)^{-2} S = 1.5 \\int (100 + t)^{-2} \\, dt = 1.5 \\left[ -\\frac{1}{100 + t} \\right] + C = -\\frac{1.5}{100 + t} + C\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\( (100 + t)^2 \\), we get:\n\\[\nS(t) = -1.5(100 + t) + C(100 + t)^2\n\\]\nTo find the constant \\( C \\), we use the initial condition \\( S(0) = 0 \\):\n\\[\n0 = -1.5(100 + 0) + C(100 + 0)^2 = -150 + 10000C\n\\]\nSolving for \\( C \\), we get:\n\\[\nC = \\frac{150}{10000} = 0.015\n\\]\nTherefore, the solution to the differential equation is:\n\\[\nS(t) = -1.5(100 + t) + 0.015(100 + t)^2\n\\]\nWe need to find the amount of salt in the tank after 20 minutes, so we substitute \\( t = 20 \\) into the solution:\n\\[\nS(20) = -1.5(100 + 20) + 0.015(100 + 20",
